---

<BaseLayout 
  title={pageTitle} 
  description={pageDescription}
  schema={articleSchema}
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
>
  <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbItems} />

  <header class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="badge-row">
        <span class="badge badge-accent">PDC MAJOR</span>
        <span class="badge">PAIRS CHAMPIONSHIP</span>
      </div>
      <h1>World Cup of Darts: The PDC's Pairs Championship Explained</h1>
      <p class="lead-text">
        The PDC World Cup of Darts provides one of the most electric spectacles in world sport. As the only pairs tournament on the professional circuit, it strips away the solitary nature of championship darts and places national pride on the line in an intense team environment.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="container content-layout">
    <main class="main-column">
      <VerificationBadge verifiedDate="October 2026" topic="PDC World Cup of Darts Rules, Purse & Finals History" />

      <!-- Section 1: What Is the World Cup of Darts? -->
      <section class="content-card">
        <h2>What Is the World Cup of Darts?</h2>
        <p>
          The World Cup of Darts is an annual international tournament staged by the <a href="https://www.pdc.tv/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Professional Darts Corporation</a>. First introduced in 2010, the competition brings together two-player national teams representing nations across Europe, the Americas, Asia, Oceania, and Africa.
        </p>
        <p>
          Unlike standard ranking tournaments where competitors play exclusively as solo competitors, the World Cup is built entirely around doubles play. Teammates share visits to the board, throwing alternate visits of three darts while coordinating setup shots, checkouts, and board management.
        </p>
        <p>
          This cooperative format generates unique psychological drama. Elite players accustomed to controlling their own rhythm must trust their partner's throwing cadence and adapt when an off-target dart disrupts a planned checkout sequence.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: 2026 Result in Brief -->
      <section class="content-card">
        <h2>2026 Result in Brief</h2>
        <p>
          England captured their record-extending sixth World Cup of Darts crown on Sunday, 14 June 2026, defeating fierce rivals the Netherlands 10–5 in the final at Frankfurt's Eissporthalle.
        </p>
        <p>
          The English pairing of world champion <a href="/players/luke-littler/">Luke Littler</a> and world number one <a href="/players/luke-humphries/">Luke Humphries</a> produced a dominant campaign throughout the weekend. After navigating tense knockout matches on Saturday and Sunday afternoon, the English duo fired a combined match average exceeding 102 to overpower the Dutch tandem of Michael van Gerwen and Gian van Veen in the decider.
        </p>
        <p>
          Defending champions Northern Ireland, represented by Josh Rock and Daryl Gurney following their maiden triumph in 2025, bowed out at the semi-final stage after suffering a narrow 8–7 defeat to the Netherlands in a deciding leg.
        </p>
        <p>
          The triumph cemented England's status as the most successful nation in World Cup history, breaking their tie of five titles with the Netherlands.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: Format Explained -->
      <section class="content-card">
        <h2>Format Explained</h2>
        <p>
          The World Cup underwent an extensive format overhaul in 2023, expanding the field to 40 nations and transitioning the entire event into a pure doubles tournament with no individual singles matches.
        </p>

        <h3>The Seedings and Group Phase</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Top Four Seeded Nations:</strong> The four highest-ranked countries—determined by the combined world ranking of their two selected players on the two-year rolling PDC Order of Merit—receive automatic qualification directly into the Second Round (Last 16).</li>
          <li><strong>The 36 Unseeded Nations:</strong> The remaining 36 teams are drawn into 12 round-robin groups of three teams each (Groups A through L).</li>
          <li><strong>Group Play:</strong> Each nation plays the other two teams in their group across the opening two days. Matches are contested as best-of-seven legs.</li>
          <li><strong>Advancement:</strong> Only the team finishing top of each group qualifies for the knockout phase, joining the four seeded nations in the Last 16.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>The Knockout Phase</h3>
        <p>
          From the Last 16 onward, the tournament operates as a straight single-elimination bracket:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Second Round (Last 16):</strong> Best of 15 legs (first to 8)</li>
          <li><strong>Quarter-Finals:</strong> Best of 15 legs (first to 8)</li>
          <li><strong>Semi-Finals:</strong> Best of 15 legs (first to 8)</li>
          <li><strong>Final:</strong> Best of 19 legs (first to 10)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Because every single match is played in alternate-visit doubles, teammates cannot rely on individual singles rubbers to bail them out. Chemistry, tempo alignment, and mutual encouragement are vital attributes for any partnership aiming to lift the trophy.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4: Prize Money Breakdown -->
      <section class="content-card">
        <h2>Prize Money Breakdown</h2>
        <p>
          The World Cup of Darts features an overall purse of £500,000. Prize money is awarded per two-player team and split equally between both competitors (£50,000 each for the champions).
        </p>
        <p>
          Importantly, because the World Cup is an invitational national event and does not feature an open tour qualifying system, prize money won does not count towards the individual PDC Order of Merit world rankings.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Finishing Stage</th>
                <th>Prize Money (Per Team)</th>
                <th>Payout Per Player</th>
                <th>Total Teams</th>
                <th>Total Stage Payout</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Winners (Champions)</strong></td>
                <td><strong>£100,000</strong></td>
                <td>£50,000</td>
                <td>1</td>
                <td>£100,000</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Runners-up</strong></td>
                <td><strong>£48,000</strong></td>
                <td>£24,000</td>
                <td>1</td>
                <td>£48,000</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Semi-finalists</strong></td>
